[
 {
  "id": "M14/p1",
  "lesson": "M14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>Which directions capture the most variation in the data?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The coordinate axes with the largest values.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>The directions of the individual embeddings with the largest lengths.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>The top singular vectors of the data matrix after subtracting the mean, equivalently the top eigenvectors of its covariance matrix.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Random directions: in high dimension they are all the same.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>The top right singular vectors of the centred data matrix. The first is the unit direction along which the projections of the points spread out the most; the next is the most spread direction perpendicular to it, and so on. This is <strong>principal component analysis</strong>. Without subtracting the mean, the first direction would mostly point at the mean, as Movement 5 shows.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "M14/p2",
  "lesson": "M14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>The centred data have singular values 30, 20, 10 and then values too small to matter. The variance along each direction is proportional to the square of its singular value. What fraction of the variance do the top two directions explain?</p>",
  "answer": "0.929",
  "answerKind": "numeric",
  "tol": 0.005,
  "options": [],
  "explain": "<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>900</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>400</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>900</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>400</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1300</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1400</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.929</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(900 + 400) / (900 + 400 + 100) = 1300 / 1400 \\approx 0.929</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">900</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">900</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1300/1400</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.929</span></span></span></span>. Squares make the top values dominate: a singular value three times smaller carries nine times less variance. Keeping two of the 768 directions keeps 93 percent of the variation.</p>"
 },
